From: Christopher Stenger <chris.h.stenger@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] media: cedrus: halt the VE before freeing its DMA buffers

cedrus_stop_streaming() frees the codec's auxiliary DMA buffers -- for
H.264, pic_info_buf and neighbor_info_buf -- and returns the capture
buffers, without ever asserting the engine's reset or waiting for it to go
idle. The driver's only reset_control_reset() is in the watchdog timeout
handler, which fires after the fact.

If the VE is still executing when that happens, it keeps writing into pages
the page allocator has already handed to something else. On this SoC nothing
can stop it: the H713 has no usable IOMMU (the node at 0x030f0000 reads zero
in every register and is disabled in DT, and the ve node deliberately has no
iommus property), so the addresses cedrus programs are physical and go
straight to memory.

That is the leading explanation for the corruption seen when decode runs
alongside a CMA-backed KMS scanout: five reproductions, five different
victims -- a pool_workqueue, the hrtimer rbtree, a dma_fence, the deadline
runqueue and a kernel stack. Those are slab and stack allocations, which are
unmovable and therefore never placed in CMA, so a write running off the end
of a CMA buffer cannot reach them. Stale-address writes can.

Pulse the reset in stop_streaming, before codec->stop() frees anything and
while the device is still powered. Gated by sunxi_cedrus.stop_reset, default
on and writable at runtime, so both behaviours can be compared within a
single boot rather than across two flashes.

Diagnostic before fix, deliberately: a reset is device-wide, so with a second
m2m context mid-job this would break that job. Making it safe means checking
that no job is running, or performing the reset from the m2m job context.

Signed-off-by: Christopher Stenger <chris.h.stenger@gmail.com>
---
--- a/drivers/staging/media/sunxi/cedrus/cedrus_video.c
+++ b/drivers/staging/media/sunxi/cedrus/cedrus_video.c
@@ -14,6 +14,7 @@
  */
 
 #include <linux/pm_runtime.h>
+#include <linux/reset.h>
 
 #include <media/videobuf2-dma-contig.h>
 #include <media/v4l2-device.h>
@@ -53,6 +54,35 @@
 
 #define CEDRUS_GUARD_POISON	0x5au
 
+/*
+ * Halt the VE when streaming stops, before anything it might still be writing
+ * to is freed (sunxi_cedrus.stop_reset, default on; writable at runtime so the
+ * two behaviours can be compared inside one boot).
+ *
+ * cedrus_stop_streaming() frees the codec's auxiliary DMA buffers -- for H.264,
+ * pic_info_buf and neighbor_info_buf -- and returns the capture buffers, all
+ * without asserting the engine's reset or waiting for it to go idle. The
+ * driver's only reset_control_reset() is in the watchdog timeout handler. If
+ * the VE is still executing at that point it goes on writing into pages the
+ * allocator has already handed to somebody else, and on this SoC there is no
+ * IOMMU to stop it (see dma_guard above).
+ *
+ * That is the leading explanation for the kernel memory corruption seen when
+ * decode and a CMA-backed KMS scanout run together: five reproductions, five
+ * different victims, all of them slab or stack allocations that CMA never
+ * hosts -- so the writes are going to stale addresses, not off the end of a
+ * buffer.
+ *
+ * CAVEAT, and why this is a diagnostic before it is a fix: a reset is
+ * device-wide. With a second m2m context mid-job this would break that job.
+ * Making it safe means checking that no job is running, or resetting from the
+ * m2m job context instead.
+ */
+static bool stop_reset = true;
+module_param(stop_reset, bool, 0644);
+MODULE_PARM_DESC(stop_reset,
+		 "halt the VE when streaming stops, before its DMA buffers are freed");
+
 #define CEDRUS_DECODE_SRC	BIT(0)
 #define CEDRUS_DECODE_DST	BIT(1)
 
@@ -654,6 +684,19 @@
 	struct cedrus_dev *dev = ctx->dev;
 
 	if (V4L2_TYPE_IS_OUTPUT(vq->type)) {
+		if (stop_reset) {
+			/*
+			 * Order matters: stop the watchdog first so it cannot
+			 * reset the engine underneath us, then pulse the reset
+			 * while the device is still powered -- pm_runtime_put()
+			 * below is what drops it.
+			 */
+			cancel_delayed_work_sync(&dev->watchdog_work);
+			if (dev->rstc_ve3)
+				reset_control_reset(dev->rstc_ve3);
+			reset_control_reset(dev->rstc);
+		}
+
 		if (ctx->current_codec->stop)
 			ctx->current_codec->stop(ctx);
 
